    14	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    15	# -------------------------------------
    16	# a) concretization_and_trace:
    17	# - The original task learns one directed legend `A -> B` next to one teal stencil,
    18	#   then every loose A pixel stamps that stencil in color B. Loose B pixels are
    19	#   inert and are either preserved or consumed depending on how colors are reused.
    20	# - This combo fuses two changes into one consistent mechanism:
    21	#   1. the execution is a color swap, not a one-way recolor;
    22	#   2. the input is only valid when that swap is demonstrated symmetrically.
    23	# - Concretely, one logical rule is a swap pair {A, B} backed by one teal stencil.
    24	#   The generator shows it as two reciprocal legends around the same anchor:
    25	#   `A B [teal-anchor] A B` along one axis. Read from left to right, the left pair
    26	#   proves A->B; read from right to left, the right pair proves B->A.
    27	# - Example trace:
    28	#   stencil relative cells = {(0,0), (0,1), (1,0)}
    29	#   swap colors = {2, 4}
    30	#   original behavior:
    31	#     loose 2 at (7,7) -> stamp color 4 there;
    32	#     loose 4 at (11,3) does not activate the stencil.
    33	#   combo behavior:
    34	#     loose 2 at (7,7) -> stamp color 4 there;
    35	#     loose 4 at (11,3) -> stamp the same stencil in color 2.
    36	# - The visible difference is therefore twofold: the legend itself is explicitly
    37	#   reciprocal, and both endpoint colors reappear later as active scattered actors.
    38	#
    39	# b) complexity_calibration:
    40	# - If the generator emitted reciprocal legends but only scattered one color, the
    41	#   task would collapse back toward the original one-way behavior. To avoid that,
    42	#   every swap pair gets repeated support in both directions.
    43	# - If the generator emitted only reciprocal legends, the old solver would often
    44	#   produce the same output accidentally by learning both directed rules. To make
    45	#   the D13 constraint observable, each sample also contains one asymmetric control
    46	#   legend with its own scattered source pixels; the combo leaves that control
    47	#   untouched, while the old one-way solver would incorrectly activate it.
    48	# - Colors are still restricted to disjoint 2-color pairs from the original
    49	#   non-teal palette, so each active color belongs to exactly one stencil.
    50	# - Output-stamp overlap is forbidden. Without that constraint, opposite-direction
    51	#   swaps could overwrite each other and hide the bidirectional effect.
